WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.644
Hi, I'm Oakum!

00:00:02.644 --> 00:00:06.381
Here is how a laser scan of one tree becomes seven pictures

00:00:06.381 --> 00:00:07.938
an image network can read.

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:11.308
Stop treating the tree as points.

00:00:11.308 --> 00:00:15.309
Render it into seven flat images holding depth, point count and height,

00:00:15.309 --> 00:00:17.310
and read those with image networks.

00:00:18.817 --> 00:00:20.312
The renderer, step by step.

00:00:21.128 --> 00:00:23.579
Centre the cloud, lowest point at ground level.

00:00:23.999 --> 00:00:27.845
Pick seven views: four sides, top, bottom, and a slice of the trunk.

00:00:28.265 --> 00:00:31.672
Project each view straight on, scale the tree to fill the frame,

00:00:31.672 --> 00:00:33.659
and drop every point into a pixel.

00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:38.458
Each pixel holds three numbers, not colours: nearest depth, point count, and height.

00:00:40.092 --> 00:00:44.447
The seven renders split into three groups: sides, top and bottom, and the trunk.

00:00:44.868 --> 00:00:47.623
Each group gets its own pretrained image network.

00:00:47.623 --> 00:00:51.067
Side features are max pooled, so no side comes first.

00:00:51.487 --> 00:00:54.479
Every tree fills the frame, so its real height is added

00:00:54.479 --> 00:00:55.568
back as one number.

00:00:55.988 --> 00:00:58.665
A small head maps it all to thirty three species.

00:01:00.383 --> 00:01:04.457
On the hidden test, the best point network scored seventy five point

00:01:04.457 --> 00:01:07.851
six, the best earlier picture method seventy nine point five.

00:01:07.851 --> 00:01:10.907
This one scores eighty five, first on the leaderboard.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.243
But each forest came from one team and one scanner.

00:01:15.243 --> 00:01:19.265
On the usual random split, these trees score ninety four point eight percent.

00:01:19.685 --> 00:01:22.894
Hold out each tree's own source for its species, and the same

00:01:22.894 --> 00:01:24.499
trees fall to sixty point two.

00:01:24.499 --> 00:01:26.371
Part of the score was the scanner.

00:01:27.642 --> 00:01:29.469
Seven pictures make a strong classifier.

00:01:29.469 --> 00:01:31.905
But test it on your own forest first.

00:01:36.025 --> 00:01:40.356
Seven renders, three networks plus height, and a big drop on unseen sources.

00:01:40.776 --> 00:01:42.824
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:42.824 --> 00:01:43.409
I'm Oakum.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:43.702
Bye!
